  <!-- Article Body -->
  <section class="bg-[#F8F8F5] py-16">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 shadow-sm p-8 md:p-12 prose prose-lg max-w-none">

        <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          Business owners have a lot on their plate, and commercial locks rarely get attention until something goes wrong. But proactive lock maintenance and replacement is one of the most cost-effective investments you can make in your business security. Here are five clear signs that it's time to call a <a href="/services/commercial-locksmith/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">commercial locksmith</a> in Dover and upgrade your business locks.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Sign #1: Your Locks Are Visibly Worn or Damaged</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Physical wear and tear is the most obvious sign. If you notice any of the following, it's time to replace your locks:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li>The lock cylinder wobbles or spins loosely</li>
          <li>The key sticks, catches, or requires significant force to turn</li>
          <li>The bolt doesn't extend or retract smoothly</li>
          <li>There are scratch marks around the keyway (potential tampering)</li>
          <li>The lock housing shows corrosion, cracks, or physical damage</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Worn lock cylinders become increasingly easy to pick or bypass. A lock that's difficult to use with the correct key is one that's likely to fail at the worst possible time — often when you're trying to lock up at the end of the night.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Sign #2: You've Had Employee Turnover</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Every time an employee leaves — whether on good terms or bad — your key control is compromised. Former employees may have copies of keys, and even well-intentioned departures leave lingering access you can't account for.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          For businesses with frequent turnover, the most cost-effective solution is a <a href="/services/commercial-lock-rekeying/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">commercial lock rekeying</a> service. Rekeying changes the internal pin configuration so old keys no longer work — without replacing the entire lock hardware. A <a href="/services/master-key-systems/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">master key system</a> can further simplify access management by giving you a single master key that opens all locks, while employees carry individual keys for their specific areas only.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Sign #3: Your Locks Are Outdated</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Lock technology has advanced significantly in recent decades. If your commercial property is running the same locks it had 15–20 years ago, you're likely missing out on key security features:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Anti-bump technology</strong> — Older pin tumbler locks are vulnerable to bump key attacks. Modern commercial locks have security pins that resist this.</li>
          <li><strong>Anti-pick pins</strong> — Spool and serrated pins make picking dramatically harder.</li>
          <li><strong>Key control</strong> — High-security keyways prevent unauthorized key duplication at hardware stores.</li>
          <li><strong>Access logging</strong> — Electronic commercial locks can record who entered and when, invaluable for security audits.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Sign #4: You've Experienced a Break-In or Security Incident</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          After any security incident — break-in, attempted break-in, theft, or vandalism — replacing your locks should be an immediate priority. Even if the entry point was a window or forced door, the locks may have been compromised or surveyed by the perpetrator.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          More importantly, locks that were defeated in a break-in (through picking, drilling, or bypass) need to be replaced with higher-security hardware to prevent a repeat. Don't reinstall the same lock that failed.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          After a security incident, we also recommend a full security audit of all access points — including back doors, loading docks, and interior restricted areas. Our <a href="/services/commercial-locksmith/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">commercial locksmith team</a> provides comprehensive security assessments for businesses throughout Kent County.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Sign #5: Your Access Control Doesn't Match Your Business Needs</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          As businesses grow and change, access requirements evolve. If you're using a single key that opens every door in the building, that's a security problem. If you have multiple departments that should have access to only their own areas, standard locks with shared keys aren't the right tool.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Modern commercial access control options include:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Master key systems</strong> — A hierarchy of keys that allows different levels of access for different people.</li>
          <li><strong>Keypad locks</strong> — Entry by code; easy to reset when codes are compromised.</li>
          <li><strong>Electronic access control</strong> — Key card or fob entry with access logging.</li>
          <li><strong>Panic hardware</strong> — Required by code for certain commercial applications; our <a href="/services/panic-bar-installation/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">panic bar installation</a> service covers these needs.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Don't Wait for a Problem to Act</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Business owners in Dover and throughout Kent County often don't think about their locks until they're dealing with a break-in, a lockout, or a security audit. Proactive lock maintenance and timely upgrades cost far less than the damage, theft, or liability that follows a security failure.
        </p>
